USAGE: oss [-h] [-n #] [-s #] [-t #] [-i] [-f]
[-h]: Throws help message: No execution.\n");
[-n]: expects integer value for max total children to launch
[-s]: expects integer value for number of max simultaneous children
[-t]: expects integer value for secons of hang time
[-i]: expects integer value for child interval launch
[-f]: expects string for log file, defaults to log.txt

EXAMPLEs:
./oss -n 12 -s 3 -t 4
./oss -s 5 
./oss -n 7 -t 4
./oss -h
./oss -n 3 -s 2 -t 7 -i 300 -f newlog.txt

COMPILE:
	Compile using make utiliy within directory containing oss.c, 
	user.c, and makefile.

RUN:
	After compiling, run program using './oss' execution with 
	appropriate usage as above.

DESCRIPTION:
	OSS program will create a number of child processes, up to S 
	simultaneous children,
	until it has created N total children. 

		OSS will then begin to operate a multilevel feedback queue to schedule all child processes
		
		oss will message these processes to pass data about the queue state and time usage.

	The child process, USER, will iterate through a loop for T number 
	of second, then close if it hits its alloted amount of time.
		
		during each loop of the worker process a random chance of entering a blocked condition or a premature termination can occur
		Oss wil handle these conditions and insert and remove from blocked queue

	Upon USER closing, OSS will continue to launch a number of
	chilren, up to S simultaneous, until N children is reached.

	Children will be created at I interval.

	Log file will be save to F.

OUTSTANDING ISSUES:
	None immediately aware of. Seems to run fine. Log file cleaner then output to screen. 

